  "slug": "colored-indexes-832d",
  "summary": "Two flow-matching preprints on apo-to-holo generation appear alongside structure-aware encoders and biophysics-informed predictors for binding, ΔΔG and catalysis.",
  "synthesis": "The clearest thread is generative modeling of protein conformational change. CrypticFlow combines Cartesian C-alpha anchors with internal-coordinate midpoints in hierarchical flow matching, and A2Holo runs dual-stream flow matching over protein language model embeddings and 3D structure, both for apo-to-holo prediction. Around this, representation and prediction work varies in how much explicit structure it uses: PS-JEPA predicts masked residue embeddings without coordinate reconstruction, BRIDGE pretrains an inverse-folding encoder on RNA backbone atoms, and SCOPE uses graph learning for catalytic residues. RAVEN and ProtBFF add physics-informed or biophysical descriptors to binding affinity and ΔΔG models. A language-model attention study predicts allosteric residues from a single sequence without structure. The common element is pairing learned models with structural or biophysical priors, or testing how far they can be dropped.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
